# FEATURE — PLAYLISTS

CRITICAL — LIVE ORGANIZATION SYSTEM

This document defines the Playlist system.
Playlists are the core structure used to organize and control live performance.

All changes must preserve clarity, flexibility, and real-time control.

⸻

USER GOAL

The user must be able to:
- organize songs in a custom order
- adapt the set in real time
- group songs logically (intro, dance, etc.)
- see what has been played
- prepare what comes next quickly

👉 Playlist = live control center

⸻

CORE PRINCIPLE

A playlist is NOT a collection of files.

A playlist is:
- a structure
- an ordered list of songId references
- optional grouping metadata

👉 No duplication of song data

⸻

PLAYLIST STRUCTURE

A playlist contains:

- ordered list of items
- items can be:
  - song references (songId)
  - group headers

Example:

[GROUP: INTRO]
songId_1
songId_2

[GROUP: DANCE]
songId_3
songId_4

⸻

GROUPS (CRITICAL)

Groups are used to structure the set.

Rules:
- groups are visual and logical separators
- groups do NOT affect playback engine directly
- groups can be collapsed / expanded
- groups can contain multiple songs
- groups may have an optional custom color
- if no custom color is stored, the default group color must remain unchanged
- songs inside a group inherit a softer visual tint from the group color
- group color is visual metadata only and must not affect playback, autoplay, or live chain logic

👉 Groups improve readability during live

⸻

FIELD FEEDBACK — GROUPS VS DEDICATED PLAYLISTS

Validated during a real concert:

- groups are very useful during organization and rehearsal
- groups help prepare blocks, variants, and set sections
- during live performance, too many groups can slow down navigation
- several dedicated playlists can be more efficient than one heavily grouped playlist

This does NOT remove or deprecate groups.

Product guidance:
- keep groups for preparation and readable set structure
- allow stage workflows that favor flatter, dedicated playlists
- avoid forcing all live organization into one large grouped playlist

👉 Groups remain a core playlist feature, but live navigation must stay fast.

⸻

ORDERING

- User can reorder songs freely
- User can move songs into groups
- User can move songs out of groups
- User can move songs between groups
- Order must be preserved automatically

Rules:
- reordering must be instant
- no lag allowed
- order must persist across sessions

⸻

DRAG & DROP (CRITICAL)

Playlist drag & drop is a live-critical interaction.

Rules:
- drag must remain fluid across multiple rows
- drag must work on real devices, not only emulator
- reorder must survive recomposition
- drag identity must use stable viewport keys
- viewport keys must NEVER depend on visual position/index
- drag must continue across swaps/recompositions
- songs can freely:
  - move inside a group
  - enter a group
  - exit a group
  - move between groups

Group boundary rules:
- group headers and group ends must not trap songs
- drag logic must provide explicit fallback behavior at group boundaries

Implementation constraints:
- playlist reorder logic must remain incremental and lightweight
- no heavy recomputation during drag
- no audio/player logic coupled to drag

👉 Drag & drop must feel immediate and natural during live use.

⸻

MOVE TO… ACTION (LIVE SAFE REORDER)

Playlist reorder must also provide a precise alternative to drag & drop.

Context:
- during live performance
- on phone screens
- with sweaty fingers / unstable conditions
- drag alone may become difficult

Rules:
- playlist must provide a “Move to…” action
- action must be available from the song menu
- movement must remain fast and predictable
- movement must preserve playlist consistency
- movement must work:
  - outside groups
  - inside groups
  - between groups

UI requirements:
- compact dialog/list
- one line per destination
- no verbose prefixes like “After:”
- left-aligned text
- compact font size
- lowercase visual style allowed for fast scanning
- long text may be truncated with ellipsis
- optimized for quick stage reading

👉 Live usability has priority over decorative UI.

⸻

PLAYBACK INTEGRATION

The playlist interacts with the Player:

- selecting a song starts playback
- next song depends on playlist order OR "Define Next"

Rules:
- playlist must not override Player logic
- playlist provides structure only

⸻

LIVE SONG FAMILIES

A Library song family can be added to a playlist as one compact entry.

Purpose:
- keep playlists flat and readable
- choose the active song/version at the last moment
- avoid duplicating several alternatives as normal playlist rows

Display:
- collapsed: one line showing the active choice
- expanded: other choices appear as compact secondary lines
- alternatives must not look like normal playlist rows

Behavior:
- tapping an alternative changes the active choice
- Play, Auto Play, Define Next, live list, and group actions use the active real songId
- changing the active choice does not duplicate audio or rebuild runtime data

Rules:
- family = Playlist/Library UX layer only
- Player continues to receive one real SongUnit/songId
- no audio fusion
- no runtime zip access
- no complex playback model

Example:
Flamenco
- Bamboleo
- Volare Gipsy
- Historia de un amor

At show time, the user can switch from Bamboleo to Volare Gipsy before Play or Define Next.

⸻

## SMP SYNC — MANUAL PLAYLIST TRANSFER

Playlists can be sent manually through SMP Sync.

Rules:
- the user explicitly selects playlists to send
- playlists remain structure only
- transferred playlist state may include order, groups, colors, and family entries
- playlist items must continue to reference real `songId` values
- no audio is duplicated inside playlist data
- no automatic deletion is allowed on the backup phone

Compatibility:
- playlist families must remain a UX layer and resolve to real SongUnits after import
- group colors and grouping metadata must stay compatible with backup transfer
- if a playlist references a song missing on the backup phone, the issue must be visible or handled safely; playback must not be used as a repair step

👉 SMP Sync transfers playlist state; Player still receives one real songId/SongUnit at runtime.

⸻

DEFINE NEXT (CRITICAL)

- User can mark a song as "next"
- This overrides playlist order temporarily

Rules:
- must be clearly visible
- must be unique (only one next)
- must reset after playback

👉 No ambiguity allowed

⸻

AUTO PLAY

- When enabled:
  - songs follow playlist order automatically

Rules:
- must respect groups and order
- must be predictable
- must not skip items

⸻

LIVE LIST BEHAVIOR

When creating a live list:

- system may create a temporary group
- current song + selected songs are grouped

Rules:
- must be intuitive
- must not confuse user
- must remain editable

⸻

LIVE CHAIN / RED GROUP (CRITICAL)

The live red group is a live-critical chaining system.

Purpose:
- create an immediate live sequence
- chain selected songs automatically
- show the next song clearly in the Player

Rules:
- songs inside the live red group must chain automatically
- playlist order inside the group must be respected
- the next song must be resolved before the current song ends
- the Player must display the readable next title
- “Prochain : null” is forbidden
- if the title cannot be resolved, use a safe fallback:
  “Prochain titre”

The live red group must never depend on:
- drag UI state
- visible row index
- collapsed/expanded UI state
- temporary viewport keys

It must depend only on:
- stable playlist structure
- songId / stable item references
- explicit group membership

👉 The red live group is part of live playback, not just playlist UI.

⸻

PLAYED TRACK HANDLING

After a song is played:

Options may include:
- marking as played (visual)
- moving to end of playlist (optional)

Rules:
- must be consistent
- must be reversible (reset)

👉 User must always know what was played

⸻

RESET FUNCTION

- User can reset playlist to initial state

Must restore:
- original order
- original positions
- remove "played" state
- preserve groups

👉 Essential for next performance

Tablet split consistency:
- tablet split is a presentation variant only
- playlist reset logic must stay unique and device-independent
- never implement separate phone/tablet reset behavior
- tablet split layouts may keep the Playlist UI mounted permanently
- therefore reset / played-state / order restoration must invalidate cached UI snapshots when the screen remains mounted
- after reset, played tracks must return to their initial positions and played markers/notches must be cleared
- phone and tablet split must both be tested explicitly after any playlist reset change

⸻

MULTI-SELECTION

User must be able to:
- select multiple songs
- assign to group
- move as batch

Rules:
- must preserve order
- must be fast and reliable

⸻

DATA MODEL

Playlist stores:
- songId references
- order
- group metadata
- optional group color metadata

Must NOT store:
- audio files
- duplicated song data

⸻

PERSISTENCE

Playlist must:
- be saved automatically
- survive app restart
- remain consistent with library
- restore optional group colors after app restart
- remain backward compatible with groups that have no stored color

PLAYLIST JSON IMPORT

Playlists can be imported directly from:
- the global Playlists screen
- the current playlist menu

Both entry points must reuse the same JSON import flow.

Import must preserve:
- groups
- group colors
- ghost occurrences / repeated `songId` occurrences
- raw song order

The Android file picker used for playlist import is based on SAF / DocumentsUI.

Known picker behavior:
- the app requests JSON-compatible files
- depending on the device, Android version, and file provider, other file types may still be visible
- this visibility is controlled by the Android DocumentsUI provider, not by playlist business logic

Restore philosophy:
- library restore prioritizes rebuilding runtime SongUnits first
- a complete Library backup restores playlists automatically after parents and virtual variants are present in the refreshed Library index
- direct JSON playlist import remains available when the user wants to import playlists individually

Restore rules:
- restored playlists must reference SMP songs by `smp://songId`
- virtual Arrangement variants restored from their parent archive must be registered as valid playlist targets before playlist state remapping
- display resolution for `smp://songId` must use the current Library index
- playback must never be required to hydrate playlist titles
- restored playlists must survive restart, device transfer, and backup restore without manual repair

Title resolution must ignore:
- null
- `"null"`
- blank

If a referenced songId is missing after restore, use a clear localized fallback instead of displaying `null`.

⸻

UI REQUIREMENTS

Must provide:
- clear list
- groups visibility
- group color customization
- drag & drop reorder
- move to… action
- quick actions (play, next, remove)

Must be:
- readable on stage
- fast
- predictable

⸻

KNOWN PITFALLS

- losing order after modification
- duplicated items
- broken group assignment
- unclear "next" state
- confusion between playlist and player logic
- unstable LazyColumn keys during drag
- viewport key collisions with URI schemes (`content://`, `file://`, etc.)
- group boundaries blocking reorder
- recomposition interrupting pointerInput
- drag-only workflows difficult on stage conditions
- red live group not chaining
- next track not transmitted to Player
- next title displayed as null
- playlist UI state used as playback truth
- group membership lost after reorder

⸻

RUNTIME SAFETY — PLAYLIST SNAPSHOTS

Playlists may be read by asynchronous tasks while they are being modified.

Examples:
- auto-backup during `MainActivity.onStop`
- backup/export
- played-state export

Rules:
- backup/export code must never iterate directly over live mutable playlist collections
- playlist reads used outside the main mutation flow must use a defensive immutable snapshot or equivalent protection
- snapshot reads must preserve the same business behavior; they must not create separate phone/tablet playlist logic
- `ConcurrentModificationException` during backup/export is a critical regression
- any new backup/export/played-state feature touching playlists must validate concurrent playlist modification

Incident documented:
- Date: 2026-06-11
- Symptom: `ConcurrentModificationException` during `MainActivity.onStop` auto-backup
- Components: `PlaylistRepository`, `BackupManager`
- Fix: `PlaylistRepository` creates defensive snapshots before playlist read/count/filter operations used by backup/export and played-state checks

👉 Runtime backup safety is part of playlist live stability.

⸻

TEST SCENARIOS (MANDATORY)

Test at least:

- create playlist
- add songs
- reorder songs
- drag song across multiple rows
- create group
- change group color and verify child tint
- move songs into group
- drag song into group
- drag song out of group
- drag song between groups
- reorder using “Move to…”
- move song into group using “Move to…”
- move song out of group using “Move to…”
- define next during playback
- auto play sequence
- red live group chaining
- next title blinking in Player
- readable next title display
- mark songs as played
- reset playlist
- multi-select operations
- verify reorder on real phone
- verify drag continuity during recomposition
- modify a playlist, then put the app in background to trigger auto-backup
- verify no `ConcurrentModificationException` during backup/export
- verify played-state and backup export remain stable while playlist modifications are happening

👉 Must be tested on real device

⸻

LIVE REGRESSION RULE

Any change touching playlist order, groups, next track, autoplay, or Player handoff must test:

- normal playlist playback
- red live group chaining
- next title blinking in Player
- readable next title display
- drag reorder
- Move to…
- group enter / exit
- app restart persistence

If any of these fail, the patch must be rejected.

👉 Live stability has priority over every UI improvement.

⸻

FINAL RULE

The Playlist must ALWAYS be:

- clear
- fast
- predictable
- controllable during live

If a change introduces:
- confusion
- delay
- loss of control

👉 It must be rejected.

## ⚠️ Playlist Title Persistence

When a user renames a title from a playlist:

- the custom display title must persist after playback
- playback must never restore older metadata automatically
- custom user metadata always has priority over imported audio metadata

Rules:
- playback must not reset edited user state
- audio source filename must never be renamed automatically
- playlist display title persistence must survive:
  - playback
  - screen reload
  - app restart

The stable identity remains `songId`.
